### **<span class="mark">Weight loss surgery in Perth: everything you need to know</span>**

<span class="mark">  
If you have spent years trying to lose weight and keep it off, you are
not the problem, and you are not alone. Obesity Surgery WA is a
specialist team of surgeons, physicians, dietitians, and exercise
physiologists who have been supporting weight loss in Perth for over 20
years. We work with you to explore your options and decide the best way
forward for *you*. Whether that is a non-surgical weight loss program or
a surgical alternative, we meet you exactly where you are.</span>

### <span class="mark">**What we mean by weight loss surgery (and why it is only part of the picture)**  Weight loss surgery, also called bariatric surgery, is a group of procedures that change the size or function of your stomach and/or digestive system to help you eat less and feel full sooner. The most common procedures we perform in our Perth weight loss clinics are the gastric sleeve (‘sleeve gastrectomy’) and the gastric bypass, with the adjustable gastric band a viable option for some people. These are all well established procedures with long track records of weight loss success in Australia.</span>

<span class="mark">Surgery is not the only pathway forward. Because
obesity is a medical condition — not a willpower failure — the right
treatment looks different for every person. Options range from GLP-1
medications and structured very low calorie diets, to dietitian-led
nutrition support, exercise guidance, endoscopic procedures, and
surgery.</span>

<span class="mark">Obesity Surgery WA is not purely a surgical practice.
We are an in-house team of physicians, surgeons, and allied health
practitioners who treat overweight and obesity as the medical conditions
they are, working together to adapt your care as your needs change over
time.</span>

<span class="mark">This page is here to help you understand what's
available, so that when you're ready, you can make a confident, informed
decision about what’s right for you.</span>

| <span class="mark">From GLP-1 medications and structured diet programs through to sleeve, bypass, and band procedures, the weight loss treatment spectrum is broader than most people realise. Many patients move through it over time, and there is no single correct starting point.</span> |
|----|

### **<span class="mark">Who is eligible for weight loss surgery in Australia?</span>**

| You may be eligible for weight loss surgery if you have a BMI of 40 or higher, or 35 or higher with a weight-related health condition such as type 2 diabetes or high blood pressure, and where diet and exercise alone have not produced long-term results. Medical weight management options including GLP-1s may be suitable if you have a BMI of 30 or higher. |
|----|

**<span class="mark">Eligibility for weight loss surgery generally
includes:</span>**

- <span class="mark">BMI 40+: surgery is generally an appropriate option
  to discuss, regardless of other conditions</span>

- <span class="mark">BMI 35–40 and a related condition such as type 2
  diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, joint disease, or
  gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD): surgery is commonly
  considered</span>

- <span class="mark">BMI 30–35: may be considered in select cases, often
  where diabetes is difficult to manage with medication alone; assessed
  on an individual basis</span>

- <span class="mark">History of genuine attempts at weight loss through
  diet, exercise, or medical means without lasting success</span>

- <span class="mark">Commitment to long-term dietary and lifestyle
  changes, supplements where needed, and ongoing follow-up:
  understanding that surgery is your starting point, and aftercare is
  what protects your results</span>

- <span class="mark">Final eligibility is always determined after a
  comprehensive assessment with your surgeon and (as needed) Obesity
  Surgery WA’s specialist care team, including medical history review
  and review of any conditions that affect surgical risk</span>

### **<span class="mark">Weight loss surgery: how it works</span>**

1.  <span class="mark">**An honest first consultation**  
    Meet your surgeon, talk through your medical history and weight loss
    goals, and ask as many questions as you like. You’ll receive clear,
    professional advice on which procedure or pathway is most suitable
    for you and your lifestyle, with no pressure to decide on the day.  
    </span>

2.  <span class="mark">**Assessment with the right person for you  **
    Depending on your personal situation, your first appointment may be
    with a surgeon, physician, or an Accredited Practising Dietitian,
    whichever team member is best placed to meet you where you are right
    now.  
    </span>

3.  <span class="mark">**A pathway proposed, not prescribed**  
    All options will be covered with you, including the likely benefits
    and potential risks and complications of each. From medical weight
    management through to surgery, we will help you understand and
    decide which is the right pathway for you.  
    </span>

4.  <span class="mark">**Treatment, with support at every stage**  
    Whether your weight loss pathway involves medication, a structured
    diet plan (VLCD), an endoscopic option, or an operation, our whole
    team is committed to and is there to support you throughout.  
    </span>

5.  <span class="mark">**Long-term follow-up, for as long as it
    takes**  
    Weight management is an ongoing process. Our team will continue to
    review your progress, adapt your plan as your needs change, and
    support sustainable results well beyond your initial
    treatment.</span>

**<span class="mark">Why a team-based approach tends to work</span>**

- <span class="mark">Assessment across the full spectrum of care means
  you are never limited to a single treatment option. Our
  recommendations are tailored to you as an individual, not a
  template.</span>

- <span class="mark">Dedicated</span> <span class="mark">Accredited
  Practising Dietitians ensure nutritional support is consistent and
  central to your care, which research consistently identifies as a
  major factor in lasting results.</span>

- <span class="mark">Our</span> <span class="mark">Physicians can help
  manage any related conditions such as type 2 diabetes and/or high
  blood pressure alongside your weight loss pathway, rather than
  treating them separately.</span>

- <span class="mark">If you begin with medical weight management and
  later decide surgery is right, that transition happens within the same
  team that already knows your history.</span>

- <span class="mark">With clinics in Booragoon and Greenfields,
  appointments and aftercare stay close to home for patients across
  Perth and the south-west.</span>

- <span class="mark">Though individual results vary and no specific
  outcome can be guaranteed, what we can offer is a thorough assessment,
  honest advice, and a team that stays with you for the long
  term.</span>

**<span class="mark">Being honest about the trade-offs</span>**

<span class="mark">Every option on the weight management spectrum
carries its own considerations, and we will never pretend otherwise.
GLP-1 medications can have side effects that need monitoring. Very low
calorie diets require supervision to be done safely. Surgery is a
significant step with real risks, a recovery period, and lifelong
nutritional commitments. But for many patients, the documented health
risks of not treating obesity are greater than the risk of
treatment.</span>

<span class="mark">Before any commitment is made, your clinician will go
through your individual situation in detail, so that whatever you
decide, you’ll be deciding with clarity and confidence.</span>

- <span class="mark">GLP-1 medications are typically taken long-term and
  can cause nausea, digestive side effects, and muscle loss, and so are
  not suitable for everyone.</span>

- <span class="mark">Very low calorie diets are an effective short-term
  tool but require clinical supervision and are not a viable long-term
  eating pattern.</span>

- <span class="mark">Surgical procedures carry real risks including
  bleeding, infection, blood clots, and reactions to anaesthetic.</span>

- <span class="mark">Surgery brings changes to how you eat and, for some
  procedures, require a lifelong commitment to vitamin
  supplementation.</span>

- <span class="mark">No weight loss pathway is passive, and weight
  regain is possible over time if the habits and support built around
  treatment are not maintained.</span>

- <span class="mark">What works well for someone else may not be right
  for you; so an individual assessment is the best way to know where to
  start.</span>

### **<span class="mark">How much does weight loss surgery cost?</span>**

<span class="mark">The cost of weight loss surgery in Perth varies
depending on the procedure, your level of private health cover, your
hospital excess, and anaesthetist fees. For patients with appropriate
private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) a
significant portion of the overall cost is covered, and Medicare rebates
apply when eligibility criteria are met. For those without private
health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, financing
options such as Zip and TLC are available, and accessing your
superannuation for weight loss surgery may be possible in certain
circumstances. Before any commitment is made, our team provides a clear,
itemised breakdown of what applies to your situation.</span>

### <span class="mark">Conversion CTA headline</span>

<span class="mark">Not sure where to start? We’re here to help.  
Alt  
You don’t need a plan yet. You just need a conversation.  
</span>

### <span class="mark">FAQs (7 questions)</span>

<span class="mark">**1. I am not sure I want surgery. Can I still come
and see you?  **
Absolutely. Uncertainty is actually a good reason to reach out. Many of
our patients come to their first appointment not knowing whether surgery
is right for them, or whether they want it at all. Our role is to help
you understand all available options, not to steer you toward a
particular procedure. You are welcome to explore, ask questions, and
take as long as you need before making any decision.</span>

<span class="mark">**2. What is the difference between medical
management and surgery?  **
Medical weight management uses non-surgical tools such as GLP-1
medications and structured dietary programs to support weight loss and
improve related health conditions. Surgery physically changes the size
or function of your stomach to produce – based on clinical averages –
greater, more sustained results for most patients. Both are legitimate
pathways; some patients use medical management first before
transitioning to surgery. The right starting point depends on your
health history, goals, and personal preference, and is something we work
through with you.</span>

<span class="mark">**3. Do I really need to see a dietitian, or can I
just have the procedure?  **
Dietitian involvement is an important part of the process, not an
optional add-on. Before surgery, a dietitian helps prepare your body and
set realistic expectations. After surgery, they guide you through the
staged return to eating and help you build the habits that support
long-term results. Patients who engage with dietitian support
consistently achieve better outcomes, and because our dietitians are
in-house, they can work closely with your surgeon throughout your
care.</span>

**<span class="mark">4. Where are your Perth clinics, and where is
surgery performed?</span>**

<span class="mark">We have consulting rooms in Booragoon and
Greenfields, keeping appointments accessible for patients across Perth
and the south-west. Surgeries are performed at [<u>Mount Private
Hospital</u>](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [<u>The Park Private
Hospital</u>](https://www.tpph.com.au/), and [<u>Waikiki Private
Hospital</u>](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/). Once your surgery is
planned, our team will walk you through exactly what to expect, where to
go, and who will be involved at every stage, so there are no surprises
on the day.</span>

<span class="mark">**5. How do I know which pathway is right for me?  **
You probably won't know until you've had a proper assessment, and that's
entirely normal. The right pathway depends on your BMI, health history,
existing conditions, weight loss goals, and what you're willing to
commit to long term. There is no universal answer, which is why we take
the time to understand your individual situation before making any
recommendations. Your first consultation is designed to give you a clear
picture, with no pressure to decide on the day.</span>

<span class="mark">**6. Will my weight loss be permanent?  **
Weight loss surgery and medical weight management can both produce
significant, lasting results, but no pathway is entirely passive.
Long-term success depends on the habits and support built around
treatment, and some weight regain over time *is* possible if those
aren't maintained. Our team stays involved well beyond your initial
treatment, adjusting your care as your needs change. The goal is
sustainable results over the long term, not just weight loss in the
short term.</span>

<span class="mark">**7. Do I need a GP referral to be seen?  **
A referral isn't required to make an enquiry or book a consultation, but
having one is worthwhile. It allows Medicare rebates to apply to your
specialist consultation fee, reducing your out-of-pocket cost for the
appointment. It also ensures your clinician has your relevant medical
history from the outset. If you don't yet have a referral, our team can
advise you on next steps when you get in touch.</span>

