    66	| Decades of experience | 20+ | Bariatric surgery in Perth |
    67	| Starting BMI | 27+ | May be suitable for the medical team |
    68	
    69	### What is weight loss surgery — and why it is only part of the picture
    70	- Section title: "What we mean by weight loss surgery, and why it is only part of the picture"
    71	- ~4 paragraphs (this page warrants a slightly longer treatment given its breadth)
    72	- Para 1: Define weight loss surgery / bariatric surgery in plain terms — procedures that change stomach size or function to help the patient eat less and feel full sooner. Name the most common procedures: gastric sleeve, gastric bypass, adjustable gastric band. Establish that these are well-tested operations with a long record in Australia.
    73	- Para 2: Establish clearly that surgery is not the only road, and not always the first. Explain that obesity is a medical condition, not a willpower failure — this framing matters because it shapes what good treatment looks like. The right answer might be medication, a structured very low calorie diet, dietitian support, an endoscopic option, or surgery — often in combination over time.
    74	- Para 3: Introduce the team composition — not just surgeons; also physicians who manage the medical side of weight, and specialist dietitians whose support is central to long-term success. Whoever the patient sees first, they are stepping into a team that can move them along the spectrum of care as their needs change.
    75	- Para 4: State the aim of the page plainly — to help the reader understand the full range of options so they can make an informed decision, rather than feeling pushed toward any single one.
    76	- Closing comparison note (below paragraphs): Give the reader a quick mental model of the spectrum: medical management (GLP-1 medication, very low calorie diet, dietitian support) at one end, surgical procedures (sleeve, bypass, band) at the other. Acknowledge that many people move between them over time, and there is no single correct starting point.
    77	
    78	### Eligibility section
    79	- BMI summary to anchor the section: "You do not need a very high BMI to start a conversation. A BMI of 27 or above, particularly with a weight-related health condition, may make you suitable to see our medical team. Higher BMI ranges are typically where surgical options are discussed. We will help you work out where you sit."
    80	- Write each of the following points — keep them accessible and non-clinical:
    81	  - BMI around 27+ with a condition such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, or sleep apnoea: medical team can review non-surgical options
    82	  - Higher BMI with prior attempts at weight loss that did not hold: surgery may be worth discussing alongside medical management
    83	  - Readiness to engage with ongoing support (dietitian appointments) as part of any pathway
    84	  - Age, general health, previous surgery, and current medications are all considered at assessment
    85	  - If the reader is unsure whether they qualify for anything: that uncertainty is exactly what an initial consultation is for — reassure them the door is not closed
    86	
    87	### How it works (5 steps)
    88	Keep steps in this order — 1–2 sentences covering the content described:
    89	
    90	1. **An honest first consultation** — the team listens to history, prior attempts, health, and what the patient actually wants; no judgement; this conversation shapes everything that follows
    91	2. **Assessment by the right team member** — depending on the situation, this may be a physician, surgeon, or dietitian first; patients are matched to the part of the team that fits where they are starting from
    92	3. **A pathway proposed, not prescribed** — the team lays out realistic options from medical management through to surgery, explains likely benefits and trade-offs of each; the patient decides together with the team
    93	4. **Treatment, with support built in** — whether the plan involves medication, a very low calorie diet, an endoscopic option, or an operation, the dietitians and clinical team stay involved throughout
    94	5. **Long-term follow-up** — weight management is ongoing; the team reviews progress, adjusts the plan as needs change, and helps sustain results over time
    95	
    96	### Benefits section
    97	- Section title: "Why a team-based approach tends to work"
    98	- 6 bullets, one idea each:
    99	  1. Assessment across the full spectrum of care means the patient is not limited to a single treatment
   100	  2. Dedicated dietitians mean nutritional support is consistent and central — a major factor in lasting change rather than short-term loss
   101	  3. Physicians can manage related conditions such as type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure alongside weight
   102	  4. If the patient starts with medical management and later decides surgery is right, the transition happens within the same team that already knows their history
   103	  5. Both Perth clinic locations (Booragoon and Mandurah) keep appointments and aftercare close to home
   104	  6. Many people experience improvements in weight-related conditions and day-to-day quality of life — individual results vary and no specific outcome can be promised
   105	
   106	### Risks — honest section
   107	- Section title: "Being honest about the trade-offs"
   108	- Intro paragraph: Acknowledge that every option on the spectrum carries its own considerations. Frame this as a genuine service to the reader — the team will not pretend otherwise. Medications have side effects. Very low calorie diets need supervision. Surgery is a significant step with real risks and recovery. The reader needs to weigh these against the documented health risks of leaving obesity untreated. The clinician goes through the individual situation in detail before any commitment.
   109	- Risk bullets to address (one sentence each):
   110	  - GLP-1 medications can cause nausea, digestive symptoms and other side effects; not suitable for everyone
   111	  - Very low calorie diets require supervision and are not designed as a permanent eating pattern
   112	  - Surgical procedures carry risks including bleeding, infection, leaks, blood clots and anaesthetic reactions
   113	  - After surgery, lifelong changes to eating and, in some cases, vitamin supplementation are needed
   114	  - No pathway works without ongoing effort; weight regain is possible if the plan is not maintained
   115	  - The best option for someone else may not be right for this reader — individual assessment matters
   116	
   117	### Cost paragraph
   118	Cover: cost depends entirely on which pathway is chosen — medical management and dietitian support look very different in cost to surgery; within surgery the procedure type matters too; private health cover, Medicare rebates, and in some cases superannuation access can all play a part; the team would rather give clear figures for the specific situation than a vague range; the best next step is to review cost guides and then discuss options directly. Do not quote specific dollar figures.
   119	
   120	### Conversion CTA headline
   121	Direction: The reader on this page is at an earlier stage than on the procedure-specific pages — they are still working out where to start. The CTA headline should validate that uncertainty as the right reason to reach out, not an obstacle.
   122	Reference tone: "Not sure where to start? That is exactly the right reason to talk to us."
   123	
   124	### FAQs (7 questions)
   125	The page must answer each of the following:
   126	
   127	1. I am not sure I want surgery — can I still come and see you?
   128	2. What is the difference between medical management and surgery?
   129	3. Do I really need to see a dietitian, or can I just have the procedure?
   130	4. Where are your Perth clinics, and where is surgery performed?
   131	5. How do I know which pathway is right for me?
   132	6. Will my weight loss be permanent?
   133	7. Do I need a GP referral to be seen?
   134	
   135	---
   136	
   137	## Tone and compliance notes
   138	- Medical advertising: no guarantees, use phrases like "clinical averages", "results vary", "your clinician will advise", "many people experience"
   139	- AHPRA/TGA: no testimonials, no before/after claims, no guaranteed outcomes
   140	- Voice: plain-language, empathetic, direct; avoid hype; this page is the broadest entry point and the reader may be at their most uncertain and guarded
   141	- Reader: a Perth adult at the beginning of their research, possibly BMI 27–35, may not know the word "bariatric", may feel shame or exhaustion — the page must normalise the conversation before it can sell it
   142	- This page should do the most to establish "obesity as a medical condition, not a willpower failure" — that framing is the emotional foundation the rest of the site is built on
   143	- Do not over-index on surgery; the medical management pathway is genuinely presented as an equal option on this page

