    39	### **<span class="mark">Weight loss surgery in Perth: everything you need to know</span>**
    40	
    41	<span class="mark">  
    42	If you have spent years trying to lose weight and keep it off, you are
    43	not the problem, and you are not alone. Obesity Surgery WA is a
    44	specialist team of surgeons, physicians, dietitians, and exercise
    45	physiologists who have been supporting weight loss in Perth for over 20
    46	years. We work with you to explore your options and decide the best way
    47	forward for *you*. Whether that is a non-surgical weight loss program or
    48	a surgical alternative, we meet you exactly where you are.</span>
    49	
    50	### <span class="mark">**What we mean by weight loss surgery (and why it is only part of the picture)**  Weight loss surgery, also called bariatric surgery, is a group of procedures that change the size or function of your stomach and/or digestive system to help you eat less and feel full sooner. The most common procedures we perform in our Perth weight loss clinics are the gastric sleeve (‘sleeve gastrectomy’) and the gastric bypass, with the adjustable gastric band a viable option for some people. These are all well established procedures with long track records of weight loss success in Australia.</span>
    51	
    52	<span class="mark">Surgery is not the only pathway forward. Because
    53	obesity is a medical condition — not a willpower failure — the right
    54	treatment looks different for every person. Options range from GLP-1
    55	medications and structured very low calorie diets, to dietitian-led
    56	nutrition support, exercise guidance, endoscopic procedures, and
    57	surgery.</span>
    58	
    59	<span class="mark">Obesity Surgery WA is not purely a surgical practice.
    60	We are an in-house team of physicians, surgeons, and allied health
    61	practitioners who treat overweight and obesity as the medical conditions
    62	they are, working together to adapt your care as your needs change over
    63	time.</span>
    64	
    65	<span class="mark">This page is here to help you understand what's
    66	available, so that when you're ready, you can make a confident, informed
    67	decision about what’s right for you.</span>
    68	
    69	| <span class="mark">From GLP-1 medications and structured diet programs through to sleeve, bypass, and band procedures, the weight loss treatment spectrum is broader than most people realise. Many patients move through it over time, and there is no single correct starting point.</span> |
    70	|----|
    71	
    72	### **<span class="mark">Who is eligible for weight loss surgery in Australia?</span>**
    73	
    74	| You may be eligible for weight loss surgery if you have a BMI of 40 or higher, or 35 or higher with a weight-related health condition such as type 2 diabetes or high blood pressure, and where diet and exercise alone have not produced long-term results. Medical weight management options including GLP-1s may be suitable if you have a BMI of 30 or higher. |
    75	|----|
    76	
    77	**<span class="mark">Eligibility for weight loss surgery generally
    78	includes:</span>**
    79	
    80	- <span class="mark">BMI 40+: surgery is generally an appropriate option
    81	  to discuss, regardless of other conditions</span>
    82	
    83	- <span class="mark">BMI 35–40 and a related condition such as type 2
    84	  diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, joint disease, or
    85	  gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD): surgery is commonly
    86	  considered</span>
    87	
    88	- <span class="mark">BMI 30–35: may be considered in select cases, often
    89	  where diabetes is difficult to manage with medication alone; assessed
    90	  on an individual basis</span>
    91	
    92	- <span class="mark">History of genuine attempts at weight loss through
    93	  diet, exercise, or medical means without lasting success</span>
    94	
    95	- <span class="mark">Commitment to long-term dietary and lifestyle
    96	  changes, supplements where needed, and ongoing follow-up:
    97	  understanding that surgery is your starting point, and aftercare is
    98	  what protects your results</span>
    99	
   100	- <span class="mark">Final eligibility is always determined after a
   101	  comprehensive assessment with your surgeon and (as needed) Obesity
   102	  Surgery WA’s specialist care team, including medical history review
   103	  and review of any conditions that affect surgical risk</span>
   104	
   105	### **<span class="mark">Weight loss surgery: how it works</span>**
   106	
   107	1.  <span class="mark">**An honest first consultation**  
   108	    Meet your surgeon, talk through your medical history and weight loss
   109	    goals, and ask as many questions as you like. You’ll receive clear,
   110	    professional advice on which procedure or pathway is most suitable
   111	    for you and your lifestyle, with no pressure to decide on the day.  
   112	    </span>
   113	
   114	2.  <span class="mark">**Assessment with the right person for you  **
   115	    Depending on your personal situation, your first appointment may be
   116	    with a surgeon, physician, or an Accredited Practising Dietitian,
   117	    whichever team member is best placed to meet you where you are right
   118	    now.  
   119	    </span>
   120	
   121	3.  <span class="mark">**A pathway proposed, not prescribed**  
   122	    All options will be covered with you, including the likely benefits
   123	    and potential risks and complications of each. From medical weight
   124	    management through to surgery, we will help you understand and
   125	    decide which is the right pathway for you.  
   126	    </span>
   127	
   128	4.  <span class="mark">**Treatment, with support at every stage**  
   129	    Whether your weight loss pathway involves medication, a structured
   130	    diet plan (VLCD), an endoscopic option, or an operation, our whole
   131	    team is committed to and is there to support you throughout.  
   132	    </span>
   133	
   134	5.  <span class="mark">**Long-term follow-up, for as long as it
   135	    takes**  
   136	    Weight management is an ongoing process. Our team will continue to
   137	    review your progress, adapt your plan as your needs change, and
   138	    support sustainable results well beyond your initial
   139	    treatment.</span>
   140	
   141	**<span class="mark">Why a team-based approach tends to work</span>**
   142	
   143	- <span class="mark">Assessment across the full spectrum of care means
   144	  you are never limited to a single treatment option. Our
   145	  recommendations are tailored to you as an individual, not a
   146	  template.</span>
   147	
   148	- <span class="mark">Dedicated</span> <span class="mark">Accredited
   149	  Practising Dietitians ensure nutritional support is consistent and
   150	  central to your care, which research consistently identifies as a
   151	  major factor in lasting results.</span>
   152	
   153	- <span class="mark">Our</span> <span class="mark">Physicians can help
   154	  manage any related conditions such as type 2 diabetes and/or high
   155	  blood pressure alongside your weight loss pathway, rather than
   156	  treating them separately.</span>
   157	
   158	- <span class="mark">If you begin with medical weight management and
   159	  later decide surgery is right, that transition happens within the same
   160	  team that already knows your history.</span>
   161	
   162	- <span class="mark">With clinics in Booragoon and Greenfields,
   163	  appointments and aftercare stay close to home for patients across
   164	  Perth and the south-west.</span>
   165	
   166	- <span class="mark">Though individual results vary and no specific
   167	  outcome can be guaranteed, what we can offer is a thorough assessment,
   168	  honest advice, and a team that stays with you for the long
   169	  term.</span>
   170	
   171	**<span class="mark">Being honest about the trade-offs</span>**
   172	
   173	<span class="mark">Every option on the weight management spectrum
   174	carries its own considerations, and we will never pretend otherwise.
   175	GLP-1 medications can have side effects that need monitoring. Very low
   176	calorie diets require supervision to be done safely. Surgery is a
   177	significant step with real risks, a recovery period, and lifelong
   178	nutritional commitments. But for many patients, the documented health
   179	risks of not treating obesity are greater than the risk of
   180	treatment.</span>
   181	
   182	<span class="mark">Before any commitment is made, your clinician will go
   183	through your individual situation in detail, so that whatever you
   184	decide, you’ll be deciding with clarity and confidence.</span>
   185	
   186	- <span class="mark">GLP-1 medications are typically taken long-term and
   187	  can cause nausea, digestive side effects, and muscle loss, and so are
   188	  not suitable for everyone.</span>
   189	
   190	- <span class="mark">Very low calorie diets are an effective short-term
   191	  tool but require clinical supervision and are not a viable long-term
   192	  eating pattern.</span>
   193	
   194	- <span class="mark">Surgical procedures carry real risks including
   195	  bleeding, infection, blood clots, and reactions to anaesthetic.</span>
   196	
   197	- <span class="mark">Surgery brings changes to how you eat and, for some
   198	  procedures, require a lifelong commitment to vitamin
   199	  supplementation.</span>
   200	
   201	- <span class="mark">No weight loss pathway is passive, and weight
   202	  regain is possible over time if the habits and support built around
   203	  treatment are not maintained.</span>
   204	
   205	- <span class="mark">What works well for someone else may not be right
   206	  for you; so an individual assessment is the best way to know where to
   207	  start.</span>
   208	
   209	### **<span class="mark">How much does weight loss surgery cost?</span>**
   210	
   211	<span class="mark">The cost of weight loss surgery in Perth varies
   212	depending on the procedure, your level of private health cover, your
   213	hospital excess, and anaesthetist fees. For patients with appropriate
   214	private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) a
   215	significant portion of the overall cost is covered, and Medicare rebates
   216	apply when eligibility criteria are met. For those without private
   217	health cover, or where out-of-pocket costs are a concern, financing
   218	options such as Zip and TLC are available, and accessing your
   219	superannuation for weight loss surgery may be possible in certain
   220	circumstances. Before any commitment is made, our team provides a clear,
   221	itemised breakdown of what applies to your situation.</span>
   222	
   223	### <span class="mark">Conversion CTA headline</span>
   224	
   225	<span class="mark">Not sure where to start? We’re here to help.  
   226	Alt  
   227	You don’t need a plan yet. You just need a conversation.  
   228	</span>
   229	
   230	### <span class="mark">FAQs (7 questions)</span>
   231	
   232	<span class="mark">**1. I am not sure I want surgery. Can I still come
   233	and see you?  **
   234	Absolutely. Uncertainty is actually a good reason to reach out. Many of
   235	our patients come to their first appointment not knowing whether surgery
   236	is right for them, or whether they want it at all. Our role is to help
   237	you understand all available options, not to steer you toward a
   238	particular procedure. You are welcome to explore, ask questions, and
   239	take as long as you need before making any decision.</span>
   240	
   241	<span class="mark">**2. What is the difference between medical
   242	management and surgery?  **
   243	Medical weight management uses non-surgical tools such as GLP-1
   244	medications and structured dietary programs to support weight loss and
   245	improve related health conditions. Surgery physically changes the size
   246	or function of your stomach to produce – based on clinical averages –
   247	greater, more sustained results for most patients. Both are legitimate
   248	pathways; some patients use medical management first before
   249	transitioning to surgery. The right starting point depends on your
   250	health history, goals, and personal preference, and is something we work
   251	through with you.</span>
   252	
   253	<span class="mark">**3. Do I really need to see a dietitian, or can I
   254	just have the procedure?  **
   255	Dietitian involvement is an important part of the process, not an
   256	optional add-on. Before surgery, a dietitian helps prepare your body and
   257	set realistic expectations. After surgery, they guide you through the
   258	staged return to eating and help you build the habits that support
   259	long-term results. Patients who engage with dietitian support
   260	consistently achieve better outcomes, and because our dietitians are
   261	in-house, they can work closely with your surgeon throughout your
   262	care.</span>
   263	
   264	**<span class="mark">4. Where are your Perth clinics, and where is
   265	surgery performed?</span>**
   266	
   267	<span class="mark">We have consulting rooms in Booragoon and
   268	Greenfields, keeping appointments accessible for patients across Perth
   269	and the south-west. Surgeries are performed at [<u>Mount Private
   270	Hospital</u>](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [<u>The Park Private
   271	Hospital</u>](https://www.tpph.com.au/), and [<u>Waikiki Private
   272	Hospital</u>](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/). Once your surgery is
   273	planned, our team will walk you through exactly what to expect, where to
   274	go, and who will be involved at every stage, so there are no surprises
   275	on the day.</span>
   276	
   277	<span class="mark">**5. How do I know which pathway is right for me?  **
   278	You probably won't know until you've had a proper assessment, and that's
   279	entirely normal. The right pathway depends on your BMI, health history,
   280	existing conditions, weight loss goals, and what you're willing to
   281	commit to long term. There is no universal answer, which is why we take
   282	the time to understand your individual situation before making any
   283	recommendations. Your first consultation is designed to give you a clear
   284	picture, with no pressure to decide on the day.</span>
   285	
   286	<span class="mark">**6. Will my weight loss be permanent?  **
   287	Weight loss surgery and medical weight management can both produce
   288	significant, lasting results, but no pathway is entirely passive.
   289	Long-term success depends on the habits and support built around
   290	treatment, and some weight regain over time *is* possible if those
   291	aren't maintained. Our team stays involved well beyond your initial
   292	treatment, adjusting your care as your needs change. The goal is
   293	sustainable results over the long term, not just weight loss in the
   294	short term.</span>
