### <span class="mark">FAQs (7 questions)</span>

<span class="mark">**1. I am not sure I want surgery. Can I still come
and see you?  **
Absolutely. Uncertainty is actually a good reason to reach out. Many of
our patients come to their first appointment not knowing whether surgery
is right for them, or whether they want it at all. Our role is to help
you understand all available options, not to steer you toward a
particular procedure. You are welcome to explore, ask questions, and
take as long as you need before making any decision.</span>

<span class="mark">**2. What is the difference between medical
management and surgery?  **
Medical weight management uses non-surgical tools such as GLP-1
medications and structured dietary programs to support weight loss and
improve related health conditions. Surgery physically changes the size
or function of your stomach to produce – based on clinical averages –
greater, more sustained results for most patients. Both are legitimate
pathways; some patients use medical management first before
transitioning to surgery. The right starting point depends on your
health history, goals, and personal preference, and is something we work
through with you.</span>

<span class="mark">**3. Do I really need to see a dietitian, or can I
just have the procedure?  **
Dietitian involvement is an important part of the process, not an
optional add-on. Before surgery, a dietitian helps prepare your body and
set realistic expectations. After surgery, they guide you through the
staged return to eating and help you build the habits that support
long-term results. Patients who engage with dietitian support
consistently achieve better outcomes, and because our dietitians are
in-house, they can work closely with your surgeon throughout your
care.</span>

**<span class="mark">4. Where are your Perth clinics, and where is
surgery performed?</span>**

<span class="mark">We have consulting rooms in Booragoon and
Greenfields, keeping appointments accessible for patients across Perth
and the south-west. Surgeries are performed at [<u>Mount Private
Hospital</u>](https://mountprivatehospital.com.au), [<u>The Park Private
Hospital</u>](https://www.tpph.com.au/), and [<u>Waikiki Private
Hospital</u>](https://waikikiprivate.com.au/). Once your surgery is
planned, our team will walk you through exactly what to expect, where to
go, and who will be involved at every stage, so there are no surprises
on the day.</span>

<span class="mark">**5. How do I know which pathway is right for me?  **
You probably won't know until you've had a proper assessment, and that's
entirely normal. The right pathway depends on your BMI, health history,
existing conditions, weight loss goals, and what you're willing to
commit to long term. There is no universal answer, which is why we take
the time to understand your individual situation before making any
recommendations. Your first consultation is designed to give you a clear
picture, with no pressure to decide on the day.</span>

<span class="mark">**6. Will my weight loss be permanent?  **
Weight loss surgery and medical weight management can both produce
significant, lasting results, but no pathway is entirely passive.
Long-term success depends on the habits and support built around
treatment, and some weight regain over time *is* possible if those
aren't maintained. Our team stays involved well beyond your initial
treatment, adjusting your care as your needs change. The goal is
sustainable results over the long term, not just weight loss in the
short term.</span>

<span class="mark">**7. Do I need a GP referral to be seen?  **
A referral isn't required to make an enquiry or book a consultation, but
having one is worthwhile. It allows Medicare rebates to apply to your
specialist consultation fee, reducing your out-of-pocket cost for the
appointment. It also ensures your clinician has your relevant medical
history from the outset. If you don't yet have a referral, our team can
advise you on next steps when you get in touch.</span>

