 Why it matters: The rewrite explains the role of dietitian support without making a comparative clinical outcome claim.
 
+4. **Residual GLP-1, safety, outcome and insurance claims remain**
+
+   a. **Dietitian research claim**
+
+   > Dedicated Accredited Practising Dietitians ensure nutritional support is consistent and central to your care, which research consistently identifies as a major factor in lasting results.
+
+   > Accredited Practising Dietitians provide nutrition support as part of the care plan and can work closely with your surgeon throughout your care.
+
+   b. **Secondary GLP-1 mention**
+
+   > GLP-1 medications can have side effects that need monitoring. Very low calorie diets require supervision to be done safely.
+
+   > Some medicines may have side effects that need monitoring. Very low calorie diets require clinical supervision.
+
+   c. **Comparative safety claim**
+
+   > But for many patients, the documented health risks of not treating obesity are greater than the risk of treatment.
+
+   > The health risks of untreated obesity and the risks of treatment are different for every person; your clinician can help you weigh both as part of an individual assessment.
+
+   d. **Secondary GLP-1 mention**
+
+   > GLP-1 medications are typically taken long-term and can cause nausea, digestive side effects, and muscle loss, and so are not suitable for everyone.
+
+   > Some medicines may cause nausea, digestive symptoms or other side effects; a clinician should assess whether they are appropriate for you.
+
+   e. **Effectiveness claim**
+
+   > Very low calorie diets are an effective short-term tool but require clinical supervision and are not a viable long-term eating pattern.
+
+   > Very low calorie diets may be used for some patients under clinical supervision, but are not designed as a permanent eating pattern.
+
+   f. **Insurance-coverage claim**
+
+   > For patients with appropriate private health insurance (typically a gold-tier or equivalent policy) a significant portion of the overall cost is covered, and Medicare rebates apply when eligibility criteria are met.
+
+   > Private health cover and any Medicare rebates may affect out-of-pocket costs, but what applies depends on your policy, waiting periods, hospital excess, current MBS criteria and the fees for your care; discuss the applicable costs at consultation.
+
+   g. **Secondary GLP-1 mention**
+
+   > Medical weight management uses non-surgical tools such as GLP-1 medications and structured dietary programs to support weight loss and improve related health conditions.
+
+   > Medical weight management uses non-surgical tools such as prescribed medicines and structured dietary programs; suitability and likely effects depend on your medical history and clinician assessment.
+
+   h. **Outcome-certainty claim**
+
+   > Weight loss surgery and medical weight management can both produce significant, lasting results, but no pathway is entirely passive.
+
+   > Weight loss surgery and medical weight management may help some people, but results vary and weight regain can occur; ongoing support and follow-up remain important.
+
+   The earlier GLP-1 and dietitian references are already covered above. Keep only active-ingredient/class names such as semaglutide, liraglutide or GLP-1 if a medicine reference is necessary; never add brand names. **Pending decision:** confirm the current TGA/legal advertising position for GLP-1 and medical-weight-management copy before publication.
+
+5. **The lower-BMI eligibility bullets are not MBS-accurate**
+
+   > BMI 40+: surgery is generally an appropriate option to discuss, regardless of other conditions
+   >
+   > BMI 35–40 and a related condition such as type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, sleep apnoea, joint disease, or gastro-oesophageal reflux disease (GORD): surgery is commonly considered
+   >
+   > BMI 30–35: may be considered in select cases, often where diabetes is difficult to manage with medication alone; assessed on an individual basis
+
+   > Eligibility is assessed individually. For a Medicare-listed bariatric surgery pathway, the relevant MBS thresholds are BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with a major co-morbidity; a BMI below 35 does not meet that MBS threshold. Your surgeon must assess the clinical options, risks and suitability for your circumstances.
+
+   This replaces the implication that BMI 30-35 is a surgical pathway and avoids treating “may be considered” as eligibility.
+
+6. **The FAQ set is missing high-intent answers**
+
+   > FAQs (7 questions)
+
+   > **8. How much does weight loss surgery cost in Perth?**  
+   > Costs depend on the pathway, procedure, hospital, private health cover, waiting periods, hospital excess, anaesthetist and other fees. Any applicable estimate or range should be discussed at consultation after your team understands your situation; no fixed cost applies to everyone. Confirm any Medicare rebate or private-cover contribution for your circumstances.
+   >
+   > **9. What is the 30/30 eating rule after surgery?**  
+   > A commonly used 30/30 approach is to avoid drinking for about 30 minutes before a meal and wait about 30 minutes after eating before drinking again. Your dietitian or surgeon will confirm whether this applies at your stage of recovery and give you the relevant eating and drinking plan.
+   >
+   > **10. Who may not meet the Medicare pathway for weight loss surgery?**  
+   > The MBS thresholds are BMI 40+ or BMI 35+ with a major co-morbidity. A person below BMI 35 does not meet that MBS threshold; a person at BMI 35-39.9 needs the relevant major co-morbidity. Clinical suitability also depends on medical history, surgical risk, readiness for follow-up and individual clinician assessment.
+   >
+   > **11. Does Medicare cover weight loss surgery in Australia?**  
+   > Medicare may provide rebates for eligible specialist consultations and MBS-listed bariatric surgery services when current criteria are met. The relevant procedure items are 31575 for sleeve gastrectomy, 31572 for Roux-en-Y gastric bypass and 31581 for BPD/duodenal switch. Private health cover, waiting periods, hospital excess and anaesthetist fees can affect out-of-pocket costs, so confirm what applies before deciding.
+   >
+   > **12. What are the 10-year outcomes after weight loss surgery?**  
+   > Ten-year outcomes vary by procedure, health history, follow-up and other factors. Weight regain or changes in related conditions can occur, so no permanent result should be promised. Ask your surgeon to explain the long-term evidence, trade-offs and follow-up relevant to the procedure being considered.
+
+   Confirm the clinic’s exact 30/30 protocol and the current MBS wording before publication. These answers contain no dollar figures; any cost range belongs in the consultation discussion.
+
+7. **The page still contains production scaffolding**
+
+   > Conversion CTA headline
+   >
+   > Not sure where to start? We’re here to help.  
+   > Alt  
+   > You don’t need a plan yet. You just need a conversation.
+
+   > Not sure where to start? That is exactly the right reason to talk to us.
+
+   Delete the “Conversion CTA headline” label and “Alt” line; retain one finished CTA only. No “Option A” artifact appears in the supplied copy.
+
